<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Novara (1513)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1513">1513</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cambrai">War of the League of Cambrai</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Novara was fought on 6 June 1513 near Novara in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, when a Swiss army defending Duke Massimiliano Sforza of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> made a surprise attack on the French army under Louis de La Trémoille and routed it. The victory marked the height of Swiss military power and expelled the French from the Duchy of Milan, until their return two years later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-novara-1513.jpg" alt="Battle of Novara (1513)" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Stumpf · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Novara_1513.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, also known as the Battle of Ariotta, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cambrai">War of the League of Cambrai</a>. The French had won the Battle of Ravenna in 1512, but the following month the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> drove the army of King Louis XII out of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> then installed Maximilian Sforza as Duke of Milan on 29 December 1512.</p><p>By June 1513 the French had occupied most of the western part of the duchy. An army of more than 20,000 under Louis de La Trémoille laid siege to Novara, which was held by Swiss troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After marching to Novara during the night, the French were surprised at dawn by a Swiss relief army of some 12,000 men. The German Landsknechte serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, armed with pikes like the Swiss, formed heavy squares and resisted stiffly, and the French managed to bring some of their artillery into action. The Swiss, however, had reached the camp by forced marches and closed on it from several directions. They took the French guns, pushed back the Landsknecht infantry and broke their squares. Caught unprepared, the French heavy cavalry could not deploy properly and fled, leaving the baggage train to the Swiss.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle was particularly bloody. The French side lost at least 5,000 men, and the Swiss about 1,500, most of them to French artillery fire during the advance. After the fighting the Swiss executed the hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechte</a> they had captured. They also took 22 French guns.</p><p>Lacking cavalry, the Swiss could not pursue closely, although several contingents followed the French retreat as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> paid them to leave. The defeat forced Louis XII to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> altogether.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Novara (1513)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Italian Wars the Confederates briefly controlled the Duchy of Milan and won the <b>Battle of Novara</b> in 1513, but their defeat by Francis I of France at the Battle of Marignano in 1515 ended their ambitions as a great power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
